## Adding Custom Colors to Your Mantine Theme

### Step 1: Create your theme file

First, create a dedicated theme file where you'll define your custom colors:

```javascript
// src/theme.js
import { createTheme } from '@mantine/core';

export const theme = createTheme({
  // Your theme customizations will go here
});
```

### Step 2: Add custom colors to your theme

Mantine requires each color to have 10 shades (from 0-9, where 0 is lightest and 9 is darkest):

```javascript
// src/theme.js
import { createTheme } from '@mantine/core';

export const theme = createTheme({
  // Add your custom colors to the colors object
  colors: {
    // Example of a custom brand color
    'brand': [
      '#E5F8FF', // 0: lightest
      '#B8E8FF', // 1
      '#8AD6FF', // 2
      '#5BC2FF', // 3
      '#2CADFF', // 4
      '#0095FA', // 5
      '#0077C8', // 6
      '#005A96', // 7
      '#003D66', // 8
      '#002033'  // 9: darkest
    ],

    // Example of another custom color
    'accent': [
      '#FFF2E5', // 0
      '#FFE0C2', // 1
      '#FFD0A0', // 2
      '#FFBD7D', // 3
      '#FFAA5A', // 4
      '#FF9838', // 5
      '#E07C1C', // 6
      '#B86314', // 7
      '#8F4A0C', // 8
      '#663404'  // 9
    ],

    // You can add as many custom colors as you need
  },

  // Optional: set one of your custom colors as primary
  primaryColor: 'brand',
});
```

### Step 3: Apply the theme in your root component

Apply your theme with the custom colors at the root of your application:

```javascript
// src/main.jsx
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom/client';
import { MantineProvider } from '@mantine/core';
import App from './App';
import { theme } from './theme';

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(
  <React.StrictMode>
    <MantineProvider theme={theme}>
      <App />
    </MantineProvider>
  </React.StrictMode>
);
```

### Step 4: Use your custom colors in components

Now you can use your custom colors in any Mantine component that accepts a `color` prop:

```javascript
// src/components/CustomButton.jsx
import { Button, Group } from '@mantine/core';

export function CustomButton() {
  return (
    <Group>
      <Button color="brand">Brand Button</Button>
      <Button color="brand.6">Brand Shade 6 Button</Button>
      <Button color="accent" variant="outline">Accent Button</Button>
      <Button color="accent.4" variant="light">Light Accent Button</Button>
    </Group>
  );
}
```

### Step 5: Access your custom colors in your own components

You can access your custom colors in your own components using the `useMantineTheme` hook:

```javascript
// src/components/CustomComponent.jsx
import { Box, Text, useMantineTheme } from '@mantine/core';

export function CustomComponent() {
  const theme = useMantineTheme();

  return (
    <Box p="md">
      <Text style={{ color: theme.colors.brand[5] }}>
        This text uses your brand color
      </Text>

      <Box 
        mt="md" 
        p="md" 
        style={{ 
          backgroundColor: theme.colors.accent[1],
          color: theme.colors.accent[9],
          borderLeft: `4px solid ${theme.colors.accent[6]}`
        }}
      >
        This box uses different shades of your accent color
      </Box>
    </Box>
  );
}
```

## Tips for Creating Color Palettes

Creating a set of 10 coherent color shades can be challenging. Here are some approaches:

1. **Use a color palette generator**:
   - [Palx](https://palx.jxnblk.com/) - Generate a color palette from a single color
   - [ColorBox](https://colorbox.io/) - Create color systems by Lyft Design
   - [Coolors](https://coolors.co/generate) - Color schemes generator

2. **Use Mantine's existing colors as a template**:
   You can look at Mantine's default colors to understand the pattern for the 10 shades.

3. **For each custom color, ensure you have**:
   - Shades 0-2: Very light shades (often used for backgrounds)
   - Shades 3-5: Medium shades (often used for UI elements)
   - Shades 6-9: Dark shades (often used for text and borders)

## Complete Working Example

Here's a complete example including a file with multiple custom colors:

```javascript
// src/theme.js
import { createTheme } from '@mantine/core';

export const theme = createTheme({
  // Add custom colors
  colors: {
    // Purple brand color
    'purple-brand': [
      '#F3EEFF', // 0
      '#E4D9FF', // 1
      '#D0BDFF', // 2
      '#BCA1FF', // 3
      '#A584FF', // 4
      '#8A61FF', // 5
      '#6F44F2', // 6
      '#5730C5', // 7
      '#3F1F99', // 8
      '#27126C'  // 9
    ],

    // Teal accent color
    'teal-accent': [
      '#E6FFFA', // 0
      '#B2F5EA', // 1
      '#81E6D9', // 2
      '#4FD1C5', // 3
      '#38B2AC', // 4
      '#319795', // 5
      '#2C7A7B', // 6
      '#285E61', // 7
      '#234E52', // 8
      '#1D4044'  // 9
    ],

    // Warm color
    'warm': [
      '#FFF5F5', // 0
      '#FED7D7', // 1
      '#FEB2B2', // 2
      '#FC8181', // 3
      '#F56565', // 4
      '#E53E3E', // 5
      '#C53030', // 6
      '#9B2C2C', // 7
      '#822727', // 8
      '#63171B'  // 9
    ]
  },

  // Set the main primary color to be our purple brand color
  primaryColor: 'purple-brand',

  // Other theme customizations
  fontFamily: 'Inter, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif',
  defaultRadius: 'md',

  // Component-specific overrides (optional)
  components: {
    Button: {
      defaultProps: {
        radius: 'md',
      }
    }
  }
});
```

With this setup, your custom colors will be available throughout your Mantine application, just like the built-in colors.

Is there anything specific about implementing custom colors that you'd like me to explain further?

