 Yes, this implementation will let you create pages for your site, though there are a few important considerations to understand:

## What This Editor Can Do

1. **Visual Page Creation** - You can visually design pages using drag and drop
2. **Mantine Component Usage** - You can use Mantine components in your designs
3. **Content Editing** - You can create and edit content in a visual, block-based way

## How to Use It for Your Site

To use the pages created with this editor on your site, you have a few options:

### Option 1: Save & Render JSON Structure

The editor creates a structured JSON representation of your page content through `editor.topLevelBlocks`. You could:

1. Save this JSON to your backend (database, file, etc.)
2. Create a renderer component that takes this JSON and renders the appropriate Mantine components
3. Use the renderer to display saved pages on your site

Example of a simple renderer:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { Button, Card, Text, Image } from '@mantine/core';

// This would use the same structure you defined in your custom blocks
function renderBlock(block) {
  switch (block.type) {
    case 'heading':
      const HeadingTag = `h${block.props.level}`;
      return <HeadingTag>{block.props.content}</HeadingTag>;

    case 'paragraph':
      return <Text>{block.props.content}</Text>;

    case 'card':
      return (
        <Card shadow="sm" p="lg" style={{ backgroundColor: block.props.backgroundColor }}>
          {block.props.imageUrl && (
            <Card.Section>
              <Image src={block.props.imageUrl} height={160} alt={block.props.title} />
            </Card.Section>
          )}
          <Text weight={500} size="lg" mt="md">{block.props.title}</Text>
          <Text size="sm" color="dimmed">{block.props.description}</Text>
        </Card>
      );

    case 'button':
      return (
        <Button color={block.props.color} size={block.props.size} fullWidth={block.props.fullWidth}>
          {block.props.label}
        </Button>
      );

    default:
      return null;
  }
}

function PageRenderer({ pageData }) {
  return (
    <div>
      {pageData.map((block, index) => (
        <div key={index}>{renderBlock(block)}</div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

export default PageRenderer;
```

### Option 2: Code Generation

You could extend the editor to generate actual React code from your visual design:

1. Add a "Generate Code" button
2. Create a code generator that converts the JSON structure to React JSX
3. Allow users to copy this code and use it directly in their React application

### Option 3: Integrated CMS Approach

For a more complete solution:

1. Create an admin area with the page editor
2. Save page structures to a database 
3. Add a dynamic page loader to your frontend that fetches the page structure and renders it
4. Add routing to serve different pages based on paths or slugs

## What Would Need to Be Added

To make this fully functional for a production site, you'd need to add:

1. **Backend Integration** - API calls to save and load page designs
2. **Page Management** - List of pages, ability to create new ones, etc.
3. **Image Upload** - A way to upload images rather than just entering URLs
4. **Routing** - A way to assign pages to specific routes/URLs
5. **SEO Controls** - Ability to set meta titles, descriptions, etc.
6. **More Components** - Expand the available components based on your site needs
7. **Responsive Controls** - Add ways to adjust layouts for different screen sizes

## Getting Started Now

With the current implementation, you could:

1. Build pages visually
2. Save the JSON output (currently logged to console) to a file or database
3. Create a simple renderer to display these pages in your actual site
4. Gradually enhance with more features as needed

